import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Wiring" description="Wiring harness, fuse box, reverse lights, push-to-start, and ECU power for LS Genesis Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Wiring</h1>
  <p>
    Wiring is the most year-specific part of an LS Gen Coupe swap. Colors and pinouts change between BK1/BK2 and 2.0T/3.8 models.
    Always verify with a multimeter and your factory service manuals before cutting or splicing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Shop manuals &amp; wiring diagrams:</strong>
    <a href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1p-_n4A5JZTp92iz3rK1sQcm1jPLSvz5W?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Drive folder (all years)</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Passenger-Side Fuse Box</h2>
  <p>
    If you want to keep factory A/C function, keep the passenger-side fuse box and the stock ECU (or at least the harness sections that feed A/C).
    Otherwise you can simplify and remove more of the factory engine harness.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/passenger-fuse-box-labeled.jpg" alt="Passenger side fuse box wires labeled: 12V, FP, A/C, CAN, Starter" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Key wires pulled from the passenger-side area. Labels match the functions below.
  </p>

  <p>Key wires commonly used from this box:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Large pink</strong> — Starter signal. Wire directly to the small stud on the starter.</li>
    <li><strong>FP (fuel pump)</strong> — OEM fuel-pump power (often red/white). Use as a trigger for a dedicated fuel-pump relay (or to the Holley green FP wire if you already have a relay). Also available near the driver's kick panel if you prefer to keep it out of the bay.</li>
    <li><strong>A/C</strong> — Factory A/C request/control wires (colors vary by year; verify with power on and A/C button pressed).</li>
    <li><strong>12V</strong> — Switched 12V sources left open for relays or future accessories.</li>
    <li><strong>Twisted red/blue (CAN)</strong> — CAN bus pair. Usually not needed for a stand-alone Holley setup.</li>
  </ul>

  <img src="/images/passenger-fuse-box-pink-starter.jpg" alt="Large pink starter wire held out from passenger side harness" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Large pink starter wire isolated from the harness — this goes to the small stud on the starter.
  </p>

  <p>
    You can either open the entire harness and pull unused wires, or simply cap extras and wrap them back up.
    Both approaches look clean if done carefully.
  </p>

  <h2>Push-to-Start / Starter</h2>
  <p>
    The pink starter wire provides 12V when you press the clutch and the start button. Connect it to the small stud on the starter.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/push-to-start-wiring.jpg" alt="Pink starter signal wire routed near battery and chassis stud in engine bay" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Pink starter signal routed in the bay near the battery — clean path from the passenger harness toward the starter or intermediate stud.
  </p>

  <p>
    On some setups the pink wire alone is not enough — you may need to ground the starter relay first, then use the pink wire to the starter.
    Push-button cars lose the factory "cut starter once RPM is seen" logic. Community solutions include an Arduino reading tach and controlling a relay, or careful Holley configuration.
  </p>

  <h2>Holley 12V Power While Cranking</h2>
  <p>
    Terminator X needs a solid 12V source that stays live while cranking.
    On the driver's-side fuse box, 2nd row, third down (labeled ECU power) is a common tap point for the Holley ECU power wire.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/ecu-power-fuse-tap.jpg" alt="Driver side fuse box with red wire tapped at ECU power location" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Driver's-side interior fuse box — common ECU / constant 12V tap for Holley power so the ECU stays alive while cranking.
  </p>

  <h2>Fuel Pump Relay</h2>
  <p>
    Upgraded pumps should run through a dedicated relay. One common scheme (OEM harness + relay kit):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>OEM Red/White → relay pin 86 (trigger)</li>
    <li>OEM Black → relay pin 85</li>
    <li>Fuel-pump black → chassis ground (seat-belt bolt works well)</li>
    <li>Fuel-pump red → relay pin 87 (switched power out)</li>
    <li>Battery power → relay pin 30</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Green and blue wires on the fuel-level sender side can be left alone.</p>

  <h2>Evap Solenoid</h2>
  <p>
    Most builders remove the evap solenoid and leave the line next to the fuel feed open to atmosphere, or disconnect it back at the charcoal canister.
    The tank must be able to vent so it does not build pressure.
  </p>

  <h2>Reverse Lights</h2>
  <h3>T56 / Magnum with reverse sensor</h3>
  <p>
    Use a reverse sensor on the transmission (e.g. Sikky short sensor, or the stock GM-style switch with a pigtail).
    A common inexpensive pigtail is the Muzzys T56 reverse-sensor harness (Amazon / similar listings, ~$10).
  </p>

  <img src="/images/t56-reverse-sensor-trans.jpg" alt="Transmission with reverse sensor wiring and pigtail kit" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Transmission with reverse-sensor lead and a basic pigtail kit ready to wire into the car.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/muzzys-t56-reverse-pigtail.jpg" alt="Muzzys T56 reverse sensor pigtail for LS1/LT1" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Muzzys-style T56 reverse-sensor pigtail — green/black leads for power and switch side.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/reverse-sensor-harness.jpg" alt="Braided reverse sensor extension harness with connectors" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Example braided extension harness to reach from the transmission up to the cabin or fuse-box area.
  </p>

  <h3>Power source at the fuse box</h3>
  <p>
    Many builders tap a fused always-on or accessory circuit at the driver's-side interior fuse box.
    One documented tap is rear-view pin/slot <strong>#12</strong> on the in-cab fuse box (wire may be grey or red/black depending on year) — verify with a meter.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/reverse-fuse-box-pin12.jpg" alt="Diagram of driver side fuse box rear view showing pin 12 tap for reverse lights" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Rear view of the in-cab fuse box (driver side) — pin/slot #12 called out as a common reverse-light power tap (grey or red/black).
  </p>

  <img src="/images/reverse-fuse-box-diagram.jpg" alt="Driver side fuse box cover diagram with fuse labels" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Fuse-box cover diagram — useful for finding a spare or known circuit (seat heater, etc.) if you prefer a different tap.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/reverse-light-harness-location.jpg" alt="Cabin harness location with arrow pointing to reverse-related wiring" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Cabin harness area near the fuse box / kick panel where reverse-related wires are often accessed.
  </p>

  <h3>Tail-light side (manual)</h3>
  <p>
    At the tail-light connector, community confirmation: <strong>gray = reverse</strong>, <strong>blue = 12V</strong>.
    Feed power through the reverse switch on the transmission so the gray wire only sees 12V when the car is in reverse.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/reverse-tail-light-gray-wire.jpg" alt="Community note: gray wire at tail light is reverse, blue is 12V" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Community note — gray wire at the tail light is reverse; provide it with power through the reverse switch and the light comes on.
  </p>

  <h2>Automatic Torque-Converter Unlock (Terminator X)</h2>
  <p>
    The Holley needs a brake-switch signal so the torque converter can unlock when you press the pedal.
    For BK2 (and similar years): run the grey wire from the Terminator X transmission harness to the <strong>red/black</strong> wire at the stop-lamp / brake-pedal switch.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/brake-switch-connector.jpg" alt="Stop lamp switch connector with yellow, red, red/black, and white wires" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Brake-pedal switch connector — yellow, red, red/black, and white wires. Red/black is the common tap for TCC unlock.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/stop-lamp-switch-diagram.jpg" alt="Factory stop lamp switch pinout diagram: YEL, RED, RED/BLK, WHT" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Factory stop-lamp switch diagram — Pin 1 RED/BLK, Pin 2 RED, Pin 3 WHT, Pin 4 YEL. Confirm colors on your year before splicing.
  </p>

  <h2>Community Notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Wire colors vary by year and engine (2.0T vs 3.8). Do not assume a 2010 2.0T diagram matches a 2014 3.8.</li>
    <li>Facebook groups "Swapped GCs" and "Genesis Coupe LS Swaps" have the densest real-world wiring photos.</li>
    <li>Budget builds often keep speedo and fuel gauge working first; tach and temp via CAN later.</li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
